// # Latest-Branch Naming Normalization (Proposal)
//
// Logline: Normalize the three coexisting rolling-pointer branch
//          conventions found by the fleet wip audit to one canonical
//          host-scoped form. Proposal only — no renames or deletes.
//
// Setup:   Evidence from the 2026-11-06 wip audit (233 refs, 97
//          repos, /tmp/wip-evidence.txt). Sibling convention:
//          autosnap.sh writes refs/heads/wip/autosnap-<host>.
//
// Beats:
//   1. The three conventions and their overlap.
//   2. Evidence they are generations, not aliases.
//   3. Recommended canonical form and migration order.

## 1. Three coexisting conventions

| Branch | Refs | Repos | Likely generation |
|---|---|---|---|
| wip/head-latest | 22 | 22 | oldest — role-named pointer |
| wip/spotlight-latest | 22 | 22 | middle — host-named pointer |
| wip/spotlight-spotlight-latest | 59 | 59 | newest — doubled host token |

Repo overlap: 37 repos carry ONLY spotlight-spotlight-latest;
22 repos carry ALL THREE; zero repos carry only head-latest or
only spotlight-latest. The newest convention covers the superset.

## 2. Generations, not aliases

All 22 triple-carrier repos hold three DIFFERENT tips across the
three conventions — they are successive generations of the same
rolling-pointer idea, not synced aliases. The doubled word in
spotlight-spotlight-latest matches a template bug shape:
`wip/<ws>-$(hostname -s)-latest` with workspace == hostname.

## 3. Recommendation

Canonical form: `wip/<host>-latest`

- Matches the sibling autosnap convention `wip/autosnap-<host>`
  (one writer per host, self-describing ownership).
- `head-latest` names a role, not a machine — ambiguous the moment
  a second lead pane exists.
- `spotlight-spotlight-latest` is the current de-facto superset
  (59 refs) but carries the doubled-token scar; renaming to
  `spotlight-latest` is a one-ref move per repo for its writer.

Migration order (each step gated, no deletes without approval):

  1. Writers: spotlight automation repoints to wip/spotlight-latest;
     head automation retires head-latest in favor of its own
     <host>-latest if it still snapshots.
  2. Consumers (reconcile.sh's wip/* match is already generic —
      verify no consumer hardcodes one convention).
  3. Hold all legacy refs until the manager signs off on deletes —
      same rule as the 31 stale pane wips in the audit.
  4. fleettick backlog: add a wip-naming drift check (count distinct
     *-latest conventions per repo; alert when >1 non-autosnap form).

Non-goal: snapshot-snapshot-<ts> dated branches are a separate,
valid family (point-in-time checkpoints, not rolling pointers).
